        {
          "block_label": "text",
          "block_content": "First solution. Note first that at 0, f/g and g are both continuous, as then is their product f. If  $ f(0) \\neq 0 $, then in some neighborhood of 0, f is either always positive or always negative. We can thus choose  $ \\varepsilon \\in \\{\\pm 1\\} $ so that  $ \\varepsilon f $ is the composition of the differentiable function  $ (fg) \\cdot (f/g) $ with the square root function. By the chain rule, f is differentiable at 0.",
